        <h2>Our AI Charter</h2>
        <p class="sub">AI is part of the information age, so it's part of this course. Rather than a single yes-or-no rule, we're clear about when it helps, when to set it aside, and how we all stay honest about using it. <b style="color:#C9D3E4">You'll write the specifics — the panels below are scaffolding to fill in.</b></p>
      </div>
      <div class="charter-grid">
        <div class="ch">
          <span class="n"><svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-check"/></svg>Where AI is welcome</span>
          <h3>When AI is a good tool</h3>
          <div class="placeholder">Add your policy: the tasks and assignments where students are encouraged to use AI, and what good use looks like here.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ch">
          <span class="n"><svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-learn"/></svg>Where we work AI-free</span>
          <h3>When to set AI aside</h3>
          <div class="placeholder">Add your policy: the moments we practice a skill without AI so you actually build it — and why that matters.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ch">
          <span class="n"><svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-eye"/></svg>Disclosing your use</span>
          <h3>How to be transparent</h3>
          <div class="placeholder">Add your policy: how students should note or cite when and how they used AI on their work.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ch">
          <span class="n"><svg class="ic"><use href="#ic-heart"/></svg>Our own transparency</span>
          <h3>How we use AI</h3>
          <div class="placeholder">Add your note: how the teaching team uses AI (e.g., preparing materials, feedback) — modeling the openness we ask of you.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
